## ABSTRACT
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) as a new class of porous materials have attracted increasing attention in the field of biomimetic catalysis. This study firstly reports a mixed valence state Ce-MOF possessing intrinsic catalytic activity towards thionine (Thi), and its application in constructing an amplified electrochemical aptasensor for thrombin detection. As noticed, the novel catalytic process combines the advantages of 3D infinite extension of the Ce(III, IV)-MOF skeleton containing large amounts of catalytic sites and spontaneous recycling of the $\mathrm{Ce(III)/Ce(IV)}$ for electrochemical reduction of Thi, thereby presenting amplified electrochemical signals. To further improve the aptasensor performance, the high selectivity of proximity binding-induced DNA strand displacement and high efficiency of exonuclease III-assisted recycling amplification were incorporated into the assay. The aptasensor was employed to detect thrombin in complex serum samples, which shows high sensitivity, specificity, stability and reproducibility. This work offers an opportunity to develop MOF-based electrocatalyst as signal-amplifying tag for versatile bioassays and catalytic applications.
Keywords: Ce(III, IV)-MOF; intrinsic electrocatalytic activity; signal-amplifying tag; electrochemical aptasensor.
## 1. Introduction
As an emerging and exciting research area of artificial enzymes, nanomaterial-based mimics have attracted considerable attention in the past decades. Since the first discovery of ferromagnetic nanoparticle (NP) with peroxidase-like activity, a great deal of excellent work on nanozyme has been carried out, such as Au (Hu et al., 2017; Liu et al., 2017), $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ (Zheng et al., 2014), $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ (Wang et al., 2017; Hu et al., 2015), carbon-based NPs (Yuan et al., 2016; Choudharya et al., 2017) and so on. Particularly, $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}\mathrm{NP},$ a classical nanozyme, is well known for its highly catalytic performance that probably is related to the coexistence of both $\mathrm{Ce(III)}$ and $\mathrm{Ce(IV)}$ . Thus, $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ -based mimics have attracted special interest in biosensors because of the remarkable merits, such as enzyme-like activities (Montini, et al., 2016; Yagati, et al., 2013), non-toxicity (Tan et al., 2015) as well as good biocompatibility (Schweiger et al., 2014; Tian et al., 2015). However, most of the above mentioned NPs are prone to aggregate, poorly showing the complete catalytic ability. To overcome these drawbacks of NPs, the introduction of surface linker is highly desirable.
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs), a new class of porous materials constructed by metal ions and organic ligands via coordination bonds, have attracted considerable attention recently. Some key features, such as 3D infinite extension of MOF skeletons, well-defined pores and exposed active sites that endows them with superior catalytic activities, have extended the application region of MOFs, including Fe(III)-based MIL-68 (Zhang, et al., 2014), MIL-88A (Wang, et al., 2016), porphyrin-based MOFs (Ling, et al., 2016) and Cu-MOF (Shen, et al., 2015) over other NPs. In 2015, Xiong and co-workers (Xiong, et al., 2015) reported that a mixed valence state of Ce-MOF possessed an excellent mimics of catalytic activity toward 3,3',5,5'-tetramethylbenzidine (TMB) and they established a sensitive colorimetric approach for the detection of biothiols. The catalytic mechanism may be attributed to the spontaneously recycling of $\mathrm{Ce(III)/Ce(IV)}$ system, which is similar to the $\mathrm{Ce(III)\leftrightarrow}$ Ce(IV) redox switch of $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}\mathrm{NPs}$ . Nevertheless, there have not hitherto been referenced in electrochemical biosensors based on a mixed valence state Ce-MOF as electrocatalyst. Very recently, our work (Wang, et al., 2017) reported that Co/Fe-MOFs exhibit highly electrocatalytic activities for thionine (Thi). Notably, different from most nanomaterials, such as Pd, Pt, $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ , $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ -based peroxidase mimics $\boldsymbol{/\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}}$ system, Co/Fe-MOFs toward Thi catalysis not only retains the amplified current in electrochemical detection but also avoids the addition of any unstable substrate in solution, making the processes simpler and easier.
Inspired by reports on a mixed valence state Ce-MOF and our previous research, in this work, Ce(III, IV)-MOF electrocatalyst as signal-amplifying tag has been explored in constructing an electrochemical aptasensor for thrombin (Tb) detection. To further improve the detection selectivity and sensitivity, proximity binding-induced DNA strand displacement and Exo III-assisted recycling amplification are incorporated into our assay. As expressed in Scheme 1, in the presence of target protein (Tb), the proximity binding of two aptamers (S1, S2) can specifically bound with Tb, leading to the displacement of S3. Relying on its rational design, S3 was introduced to open the capture probe 1 (CP1), resulting in the duplex DNAs. Subsequently, with the aid of Exo III, the DNA duplex was digested from $3^{\prime}$ blunt terminus, leading to the S3 droping from the DNA duplex to initiate the next cycle. As a result, with the proceeding of the Exo III-assisted S3 recycling, a large amount of single-stranded capture probes (partial CP1 sequences) are produced. Eventually, Au-Thi-Au@Ce(III, IV)-MOF labeled hairpin probe 1 (HP1) can be introduced to the sensing interface via binding with the above CP1 sequences, and thus, a significantly amplified current is obtained. Notably, the class of mixed valence state MOFs as electrocatalyst highlights the high catalytic activity and spontaneous recycling of the $\mathrm{Ce(III)/Ce(IV)}$ system for dye molecules catalysis even without substrate. The designed aptasensor is employed to detect Tb in complex serum samples down to the low fM level.
## 2. Experimental
## 2.1. Reagent and materials
Thrombin (Tb) and exonuclease III (Exo III) were obtained from New England Biolabs (Ipswich, USA). Gold chloride $\mathrm{(HAuCl_{4})}$ ), bovine serum albumin (BSA), bovine hemoglobin (BHB), L-cysteine (L-Cys), polyethyleneimine (PEI) and thionine (Thi) were purchased from Sigma Chemical Co. (St. Louis, USA). $\mathrm{Ce}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , 1,3,5-benzenetricarboxylic acid $\mathrm{(H_{3}B T C)}$ , NaOH, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{2}\ (30\ \mathrm{\w}t\%)$ and anhydrous ethanol were received from Shanghai Chemical Reagents Corporation (Shanghai, China). 3,3',5,5'-tetramethylbenzidine (TMB) was obtained from Aladdin.
The buffers involved in this work were prepared as follows: HAc-NaAc $(0.1\mathbf{M}$ ， pH 5.5) was used as detection buffer for SWV measurement. 0.1 M phosphate-buffered saline (PBS, $\mathrm{pH}~7.4)$ was prepared by using ${\mathrm{Na}}_{2}{\mathrm{HPO}}_{4}$ , ${\mathrm{KH}}_{2}{\mathrm{PO}}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{KCl.5\mM\[Fe(CN)_{6}]^{4-/3-}}$ solution containing $0.1\mathbf{M}$ KCl was employed as a redox probe for cyclic voltammetry (CV) and electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) characterizations. Probe immobilization buffer (IB, pH 7.4) contained $10~\mathrm{\mM}$ Tris-HCl, $10\ \mathrm{mM}$ tris (2-carboxyethyl) phosphine hydrochloride (TCEP), $1.0\ \mathrm{mM}$ ethylenediaminetetraacetic acid (EDTA) and 0.1 M NaCl. DNA hybridization buffer (HB, pH 7.4) contained $10~\mathrm{mM}$ Tris-HCl, 1.0 mM EDTA and 1.0 M NaCl. Ultrapure
water was used throughout this study. All the oligonucleotides were synthesized and purified by Sangon Biological Engineering Technology and Services Co., Ltd. (Shanghai, China) and the sequences were listed as follows:   
S1: 5'-GGTTGGTGTGGTTGGTTTTTTTCGGGTGTGGGACGTTT-3'   
S2: $5^{\prime}$ -CGTCCCACACCCGATTTTTTAGTCCGTGGTAGGGCAGGTTGGGGTGACT-3' S3: $5^{\prime}$ -CGTCCCACACCCGATTTAACCACC-3'   
Capture probe 1 (CP1): 5'-SH-TCCCGTCCCACATTCCTTAAATCGGGTGTGGGACG-3' Hairpin probe 1 (HP1): 5'-SH-TTTCCCGTCCCGGAATGTGGGACG-3'
## 2.2. Apparatus
CV and square wave voltammetry (SWV) measurements were carried out with a CHI660E electrochemical workstation (Shanghai Chen Hua Instrument, Co., China). EIS characterization was performed with a PARSTAT 4000 electrochemical workstation (Princeton Applied Research, America). All experiments were performed using a three-electrode system with the modified glassy carbon electrode (GCE, $\varPhi=4$ mm) as working electrode, a saturated calomel electrode (SCE) as reference electrode and a platinum wire as auxiliary electrode, respectively. For characterization, scanning electron microscopy (SEM, Carl Zeiss Microscopy GmbH., Gemany) and X-ray photoelectron spectra (XPS, Thermo, USA) were used.
## 2.3. Synthesis of Ce(III, IV)-MOF
Ce(III, IV)-MOF was prepared according to a facile in sit artial oxidation Ce-MOF (Xiong, et al., 2015). Briefly, 0.105 g $\mathrm{{H_{3}B T C}}$ $\mathrm{70.5~mM},$ was dissolved in 5 mL water-ethanol solution $\left(\nu/\nu{=}1;1\right)$ ), at the same time, $0.217\textrm{g C e}(\mathrm{NO_{3}})_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H_{2}O}$ (0.5 mM) was dissolved in $5~\mathrm{mL}$ ultrapure water. After that, the $\mathrm{Ce}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ solution was added to the $\mathrm{{H_{3}B T C}}$ solution dropwisely under vigorous stirring and was kept in water bath at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{\textrm{1h}}$ . After the reaction, the white precipitate was obtained by centrifugation and rinsing with ultrapure water, followed by drying at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . In this way, the white product of Ce-MOF was obtained. Later on, $30~\mathrm{mg}$ of the above Ce-MOF was resolved in 6 mL ultrapure water with ultrasonic dispersion. Subsequently, $75~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of fresh NaOH and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ mixed solution was added for in situ partial oxidation. After reaction for $5~\mathrm{{min}}$ , the suspension turned yellow. Finally, the product of $\mathrm{Ce(III,IV)}$ -MOF was collected by centrifugation and dried under vacuum at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .
## 2.4. Preparation of the HP1 bioconjugate (HP1/Au-Thi-Au@Ce(III, IV)-MOF)
The HP1 bioconjugate was synthesized according to the steps as following. Firstly, the obtained Ce(III, IV)-MOF (1 mg) was dissolved in ultrapure water (1 mL) containing $20~\ensuremath{\upmu\mathrm{L}}$ PEI $(1\%)$ and stirred for 4 h at room temperature. After centrifugation at 12000 rpm for 5 min to remove the unreacted PEI, the resulting precipitate of PEI modified Ce(III, IV)-MOF was resuspended in 1 mL PBS. At the same time, gold nanoparticles (AuNPs) were prepared according to the previous protocol (Ambrosi, et al., 2007). Afterwards, $5~\mathrm{mL}$ as-prepared AuNPs and 1 mL Thi $(30~\mathrm{mM})$ were added into the obtained PEI modified Ce(III, IV)-MOF solution under magnetic stirring for $^{4\mathrm{~h~}}$ . Subsequently, the obtained Thi-Au@Ce(III, IV)-MOF was collected by centrifugation and then dispersed in PBS. After that, 5 mL AuNPs were added into the Thi-Au@Ce(III, IV)-MOF again for the formation of
Au-Thi-Au@Ce(III, IV)-MOF. Further, the product above was redispersed in $1~\mathrm{mL}$ PBS containing $100~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ HP1 $(10~\upmu\mathbf{M})$ to react overnight under soft stirring at $4~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Finally, the HP1 bioconjugate (HP1/Au-Thi-Au $@$ Ce(III, IV)-MOF) was successfully prepared.
## 2.5. Fabrication of the aptasensor
Firstly, to obtain a mirror-like surface, GCE was carefully polished with alumina powder, followed by sonication with ultrapure water. After dried in the air, the cleaned GCE was immersed into $1\%\mathrm{HAuCl}_{4}$ solution for electrochemical deposition at $-0.2\mathrm{V}$ for 30 s to obtain a thin AuNPs layer modified GCE. Next, 10 μL CP1 (10 μM, IB) was bounded to the DpAu/GCE through Au-S affinity. Thereafter, $10~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ BSA $(1\%)$ solution was dropped onto the CP1/DpAu/GCE for 0.5 h to block the remaining active sites. After every modifying step, the modified electrode was washed gently with ultrapure water to avoid physical adsorption. Finally, the resulting electrode (BSA/CP1/DpAu/GCE) was stored at $4^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ when not in use.
## 2.6. Experimental measurements
Tb assay procedure: Initially, to obtain $10\upmu\mathrm{M}$ of the partial DNA duplex (S1/S3), the mixture of $10\upmu\mathrm{L}$ S1 ( $10\upmu\mathrm{M}$ , HB) and $10~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ S3 ( $10~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ , HB) were heated to $95^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $5~\mathrm{min}$ and cooled down to room temperature naturally. The mixture was then distributed in various microcentrifuge tubes, to which $1~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ S2 ( $10~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ , HB) and different concentrations of Tb were added for reaction under incubation at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 1 h. After that, $10~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ reacted solution (containing $10~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ S1, $10~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ S3 and Tb with different concentrations) was dropped onto the BSA/CP1/DpAu/GCE and incubated in incubator at $37~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $2\mathrm{~h~}$ to obtain S3/BSA/CP1/DpAu/GCE. Next, the resultant aptasensor was incubated with the mixture of $1~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ Exo III (200 U, HB) and $10~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ Exo III buffer at $37~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $2\mathrm{~h~}$ to accomplish the Exo III-assisted recycling reaction. Finally, $15~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ HP1 bioconjugate (HP1/Au-Thi-Au@Ce(III, IV)-MOF) was dropped onto the surface of the modified electrode. The electrode was rinsed with ultrapure water slightly after each step.
All measurements were operated at a CHI660E electrochemical workstation. The modified electrodes from each steps were characterized by CV and EIS in 0.1 M PBS $\mathrm{(pH~7.4)}$ containing 5 mM $\left[\mathrm{Fe(CN)}_{6}\right]^{4-73}$ -. CV experiment was performed from -0.2 V to $0.6\mathrm{V}$ at a scan rate of $0.1\mathrm{~V~s~}^{-1}$ . EIS was determined at a frequency window from 0.01 Hz to $100~\mathrm{{KHz}}$ and the alternative voltage of 5 mV. SWV characterization was carried out in 0.1 M HAc-NaAc buffer $\mathrm{(pH}5.5)$ at a potential range from 0.2 to -0.5V (vs. SCE).
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Characterization of Ce(III, IV)-MOF
SEM was used to investigate the size and the surface morphology of Ce(III, IV)-MOF. As can be seen from Fig. 1A, the as-prepared Ce-MOF exhibits an aligned firewood structure with an average size of about $600\ \mathrm{nm}$ . The morphology of Ce(III, IV)-MOF is similar to the parent Ce-MOF with a rougher surface as shown in Fig. 1B. While, the photographs and crystal structure diagram in Fig. S1 show that the white Ce(III)-MOF transformed into the yellow Ce(III, IV)-MOF, indicating a change in the oxidation state from $\mathrm{Ce(III)}$ to $\mathrm{Ce(IV)}$ . From Fig. 1C, we can see that numerous
AuNPs are randomly attached onto the surface of Ce(III, IV)-MOF. Further, XPS was employed to prove the successful synthesis of Ce(III, IV)-MOF. In Fig. S2, characteristic peaks for Ce 3d5, C 1s, O 1s regions can be clearly observed in the fully scanned XPS peaks of Ce(III, IV)-MOF. The Ce 3d5 regions in Fig. 1D, the peaks at 882.68, 898.88, 901.28 and 917.08 eV are associated with $\mathrm{Ce(IV)}$ , while the peaks at 885.78 and 904.08 eV are associated with $\mathrm{Ce(III)}$ (Xiong, et al., 2015). The O 1s doublet $(529.68\mathrm{eV}$ and $531.48\mathrm{eV}$ for the metal oxide and $\scriptstyle\mathrm{C=O/OH}$ ) (Li, et al., 2014a) and the C 1s doublet $(284.80\ \mathrm{eV}$ and $288.50\ \mathrm{eV}$ for the C-C and $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=O}}$ respectively) (Lu, et al., 2016) are also displayed in Fig. S2. These results indicate the successful formation of the Ce(III, IV)-MOF.
## 3.2. Electrochemical behaviors of the aptasensor
The stepwise assembling process of the proposed electrode was characterized by electrochemical measurements of CV and EIS in $5.0\ \mathrm{mM}$ $\mathrm{[Fe(CN)_{6}]}^{4-/3-}$ solution containing 0.1 M KCl. The bare GCE exhibits a pair of the apparent redox peak of $\mathrm{[Fe(CN)_{6}]}^{4-/3-}$ with a peak value of current response about $193~\upmu\mathrm{A}$ (Fig. 2A, curve a). When a thin layer of AuNPs is electrochemically deposited on the bare GCE, the redox peak current obviously increases to $224~\upmu\mathrm{A}$ (Fig. 2A, curve b), which is attributed to the fact that AuNPs have good conductivity and large surface area to accelerate the electron transfer effectively. After modification with CP1, CV response decreases significantly (Fig. 2A, curve c). Followed by blocking with BSA, a dramatic decrease in redox current can be obtained (Fig. 2A, curve d). Finally, after the aptasensor is incubated with S3 ( $10\upmu\mathrm{M})$ , CV response is decreased again (Fig. 2A, curve e). The reason for the decrease is that the non-electroactive CP, BSA and S3 layers will prevent the electron from approaching the electrode surface. As another powerful tool for characterizing the interface properties of electrodes, EIS was also employed to further provide information about the fabricated process. As shown in Fig. 2B, in the Nyquist plots, the semicircle diameter of EIS is equal to $R_{e t}$ . A small semicircle is observed on bare GCE (Fig. 2B, curve a), implying the good electrical conductivity of the electrode surface. When AuNPs are modified on the GCE, the semicircle is disappeared and a straight line appeared (Fig. 2B, curve b), which can be ascribed to the excellent conductivity of AuNPs. Subsequently, when CP1, BSA and Tb were successively immobilized, the semicircle appears again and the diameter increases in order (Fig. 2B, curves c, d and e). The reason for this is that the formation of DNA sequence and protein molecules layers hinder the electron transfer. As a result, EIS results are in good accordance with CV measurements, implying that the proposed aptasensor is successfully constructed.
## 3.3. Optimization of aptasensor preparation conditions
To achieve the best performance of the aptasensing system, the experimental conditions including the concentration of CP1 and Exo III, and the incubation temperature of Exo III were optimized. As shown in Fig. 3A, the SWV response increases with the increasing concentration of CP1 from 0.1 to $1~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ , and thereafter remains almost unchanged, which indicates an optimal CP1 concentration of $1~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ .
Fig. 3B depicts the increasing peak current when the Exo III concentration changes from 0.5 to $2.5\mathrm{~U~}\upmu\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ with the maximal current response occurs at $2.0\mathrm{~U~}\upmu\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ . Moreover, the incubation temperature of Exo III also plays a key role for the efficiency of aptasensor. As shown in Fig. 3C, it is obvious that the peak current increases with increasing temperature from $20~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to $37\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . However, the SWV response decreases when the temperature increases from $37~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to $55~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Therefore, the optimized incubation temperature of Exo III is $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for the further investigation.
## 3.4. Performance of the electrochemical aptasensor
## 3.4.1. Comparison of different signal amplification strategies
To demonstrate the electrocatalytic activity of the Ce(III, IV)-MOF towards Thi and Exo III-assisted recycling amplification of the aptasensor, different amplification strategies were used for the determination of Tb ( $\mathbf{\Omega}^{\mathrm{i0\nM}},$ ). As shown in Fig. S3, after the resultant electrode (S1+S3+S2+Tb/BSA/CP1/AuNPs/GCE) is only incubated with HP1/Au-Thi, a weak current signal is yielded (curve a). In order to confirm the Exo III-assisted recycling amplification, the resultant electrode is incubated with both Exo III and HP1/Au-Thi, and an obvious amplified SWV signal is obtained (curve b). Such comparison clearly suggests that Exo III-assisted recycling amplification certainly can amplify the peak current. Compared with the above mentioned curve (a), when the HP1/Au-Thi is changed to the HP1/Au-Thi-Au@Ce(III, IV)-MOF, a remarkably increased SWV signal is produced (curve c), implying the highly catalytic activity of Ce(III, IV)-MOF towards Thi. As expected, when the resultant electrode is incubated with Exo III and HP1/Au-Thi-Au@Ce(III, IV)-MOF simultaneously, the maximum response can be obtained (curve d), which is three times more than that of curve (a). Therefore, these comparison results clearly indicate that the high electrocatalytic activity of Ce(III, IV)-MOF for electrochemical reduction of Thi and Exo III-assisted recycling amplification can be effectively used to amplify the detection sensitivity.
## 3.4.2. SWV responses and calibration curves
To investigate the analytical performance of the proposed method, the sensitivity and the quantitative detection of Tb was evaluated with different concentrations of Tb under the optimized conditions. Fig. 4A shows that the prepared aptasensor depicts gradually increasing SWV response with the increasing concentration of Tb (Fig. 4A, curves a–h). Corresponding to the SWV response of Fig. 4A, the calibration curve is shown in Fig. 4B, it is found that the peak current is proportional to the logarithmic of Tb concentration. A calibration curve is obtained with the equation of $I\left(\upmu\mathrm{A}\right)=2.543$ lgc $\mathrm{(nM)}+48.72$ $(r=0.994)$ from 0.1 fM to $10\ \mathrm{nM}$ , and the detection limit is determined to be 0.06 fM through the 3σ rule. Results suggest that the proposed aptasensor can be employed to detect Tb quantitatively with a low detection limit, attributing to the electrocatalytic activity of Ce(III, IV)-MOF and Exo III-assisted recycling amplification. Furthermore, the analytical performance compared with other methods for Tb detection was listed in Table S1 (Huang et al., 2015; Li et al., 2014b; Ling et al., 2015; Piscitellia et al., 2017; Wang et al., 2016; Yang et al., 2017; Yuan et al., 2012; Zhao et al., 2008; Zhao et al., 2014; Zheng et al., 2016). It is clear that the prepared aptasensor exhibits a wider linear range and a lower detection limit, which may hold a new promise for highly sensitive bioassays applied in Tb detection.
## 3.4.3. Specificity, stability and reproducibility of the aptasensor
Specificity is a significant criterion to evaluate the performance of the aptasensor. The interfering substances, including L-Cys, BSA, BHB, were examined to access the selectivity of the proposed aptasensor. It can be seen in Fig. 5A, when 10-fold L-Cys, BSA and BHB are used to replace the target Tb, respectively, the changes of the SWV signal are negligible compared to the presence of lower concentrated Tb. Besides, no remarkable current change can be observed toward the mixture of all these interfering substances (each at $100\ \mathrm{nM},$ in the target Tb $(10\ \mathrm{\hat{~}n M})$ , thereby suggesting the specificity of the developed method.
To assess the stability of the obtained aptasensor, intermittent SWV scanning repeated 12 times were tested. As presented in Fig. 5B, no obvious change in SWV response is observed $(R S D=0.93\%)$ . Further, the long-term storage stability was investigated by using a batch of prepared aptasensors (BSA/CP1/DpAu/GCE), Tb and HP1/Au-Thi-Au@Ce(III, IV)-MOF stored at $4~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and measured over a period of 10 days. Every 2-5 days, the above electrodes was taken out and then incubated with 10 $\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of Tb and $15~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of HP1 bioconjugates, successively. It can be seen in Fig. 5C, no apparent current difference is found during 10 days, verifying an acceptable storage stability of the aptasensor.
In addition, the reproducibility is also of significance for estimating the performance of the aptasensor. Thus, intra- and interassays are valuated oward $10\mathrm{nM}$ of Tb, and the measurement results are shown in Fig. 5D. The relative standard deviation (RSD) of intra- and inter-assays are found to be $4.11\%$ and $4.42\%$ , respectively, indicating a desirable reproducibility of the proposed aptasensor.
## 3.4.4. Analysis of Tb in human serum samples
To further test the feasibility of the proposed aptasensor in the detection of real samples, the recovery experiments were performed by adding different concentrations of Tb into 100-fold diluted human serums (provided by People's Hospital of Shaanxi Province, China). The results of five parallel examinations are listed in Table S2, and the range of the recovery is from $95.1\%$ to $110.0\%$ , which demonstrates the applicability of the proposed aptasensor for the analysis of the target Tb in real samples.
## Conclusions
In summary, a new dual amplification strategy integrated Ce(III, IV)-MOF with Exo III-assisted recycling amplification was applied for sensitive Tb detection. Using Ce(III, IV)-MOF as signal-amplifying tag, the major merits of such amplified sensor presents the high-performance catalytic activity for Thi even in the absence of substrate, which simplifies the detection process and provides the precise detection signal. Additionally, Exo III-assisted recycling amplification is introduced to further enhance the detection sensitivity. With these advantages, the developed aptasensor, sensitive to the target Tb as low as 0.06 fM, exhibits highly selective against non-target proteins and long-term storage stability, holding great potential in clinical application. However, the aptasensor construction requires multiple steps, so our further work is to simplify the modification steps and make the whole strategy more facile and convenient.
